Here's how to handle each scenario in MSTest:

### 1. Windows-only tests

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[OSCondition(OperatingSystems.Windows)]
// Or, if using built-in MSTest v3+:
[SupportedOSPlatform("windows")]
public void Registry_ReadsValue()
{
    // Windows registry code
}
```

If your framework lacks `OSCondition`, use a manual check:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Registry_ReadsValue()
{
    if (!OperatingSystem.IsWindows())
    {
        Assert.Inconclusive("Requires Windows.");
        return;
    }
    // ...
}
```

### 2. Skip in CI (requires local GPU)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Render_UsesGpu()
{
    if (Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("CI") is not null)
    {
        Assert.Inconclusive("Skipped in CI — requires local GPU.");
        return;
    }
    // GPU test logic
}
```

Or define a reusable attribute (MSTest v3+):

```csharp
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method)]
public class SkipInCIAttribute : Attribute, ITestDataSource { /* ... */ }
```

### 3. Flaky external API — retry with `[Timeout]`

MSTest doesn't have a built-in retry. Common patterns:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[Timeout(10000)] // fail fast if the API hangs
public void Weather_ReturnsForecast()
{
    var result = CallWithRetry(() => _weatherApi.GetForecast(), maxRetries: 3);
    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
}

private T CallWithRetry<T>(Func<T> action, int maxRetries)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < maxRetries; i++)
    {
        try { return action(); }
        catch when (i < maxRetries - 1)
        {
            Thread.Sleep(500 * (i + 1)); // exponential-ish backoff
        }
    }
    return action(); // final attempt throws naturally
}
```

> **Better long-term fix:** Mock the weather API in unit tests and only hit it in integration tests.

### 4. Cleanup with `[TestCleanup]` (runs even on failure)

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class TempFileTests
{
    private string _tempFile = null!;

    [TestInitialize]
    public void Setup()
    {
        _tempFile = Path.GetTempFileName();
    }

    [TestCleanup] // runs after EVERY test, even on failure/exception
    public void Cleanup()
    {
        if (File.Exists(_tempFile))
            File.Delete(_tempFile);
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void ProcessFile_WritesOutput()
    {
        File.WriteAllText(_tempFile, "data");
        // test logic — Cleanup runs regardless of outcome
    }
}
```

For class-level setup/teardown, use `[ClassInitialize]` / `[ClassCleanup]`.

**Summary:**

| Scenario | Approach |
|---|---|
| OS-specific | `Assert.Inconclusive()` guard or `[SupportedOSPlatform]` |
| Skip in CI | Check `CI` env var + `Assert.Inconclusive()` |
| Flaky API | Retry helper + `[Timeout]`; prefer mocks |
| Cleanup | `[TestCleanup]` — guaranteed to run |
